<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable evaluation and critique capabilities, providing insightful feedback and identifying flaws in various tasks. However, limited research has explored which types of critiques are most effective for improving model responses or how to generate such critiques.
To address this gap, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>efinement-oriented <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ritique <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization (RCO), a novel framework designed to train critic models using refinement signals.
RCO uses a feedback loop where critiques, generated by the critic model, guide the actor model in refining its responses. The critique utility (CU) quantifies the effectiveness of these refinements, serving as the reward signal for training the critic model. By focusing on critiques that lead to better refinements, RCO eliminates the need for direct critique preference assessment, ensuring that critiques driving meaningful improvements are rewarded.
We evaluate RCO across five tasks, i.e., dialog generation, summarization, question answering, mathematical reasoning, and code generation, and show that it significantly outperforms traditional methods and open-source models in terms of critique quality and refinement outcomes. Our contributions include the introduction of RCO, a novel supervision scheme based on refined response preferences, and comprehensive experimental results that highlight the method’s effectiveness in enhancing LLM critique-refinement loops.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        We release the code and data at https://github.com/publicstaticvo/critique.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The critique ability, defined as the capacity to identify and refine flaws in responses, is crucial for improving the reliability of automatic evaluation and enabling the self-improvement of large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saunders et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Enhancing this ability is challenging, as it requires LLMs to not only detect errors but also engage in a deeper analysis of user queries and responses, extending beyond superficial criticism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Recent studies have sought to address this challenge of LLMs via human-curated critique datasets and alignment strategies like supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2024)</a></cite> and reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">McAleese et al. (2024)</a></cite>, yielding promising results in identifying and rectifying shortcomings across various tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22157v1/fig1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/205;" width="476" height="205" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>A comparison between previous methods, which train critic models via direct critique preference judgment and are not helpful for refinement, and our method, which train critic models by converting preferences of refined responses into critique reward values and is helpful for refinement.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, current methods primarily train models to generate critiques for evaluation purposes, rather than linking critique to refinement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.
This disjointed approach fails to connect error identification with output improvement, limiting the practical value of critiques.
While the critique-refinement paradigm is explored in LLM alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wadhwa et al. (2024)</a></cite> and reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>,
existing works lack systematic investigation on how critique enhancements can lead to meaningful refinements in responses.
Without this link, assessing critique quality remains challenging
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Addressing this gap is essential for advancing both LLM evaluation and self-improvement capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we introduce Refinement-oriented Critique Optimization (RCO), a novel training paradigm for critic models that uses critique utility (CU) as a reward signal, which is calculated by comparing the refined responses to the initial ones.
This approach encourages the generated critiques
to drive substantial improvements in the output, creating a more effective critique-refinement loop.
Specifically, RCO feeds critiques and initial responses into the actor model and prompts it to generate multiple refined responses.
CU is then quantified as the proportion of refinements preferred over the initial response, which serves as the reward signal for training the critic model.
By focusing on the refinement outcomes, RCO eliminates the need for direct critique preference assessment and ensures that critiques leading to better refinements are rewarded.
We evaluate RCO across five tasks, i.e., dialog generation, summarization, question answering, mathematical reasoning, and code generation, using baseline models, models trained with critique preferences, and advanced open-source models. Experimental results demonstrate that RCO significantly enhances critic model performance, outperforming existing methods across multiple benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold:
(1) We propose RCO, a method that prioritizes critiques that facilitate effective refinement of actor model responses, addressing limitations of previous approaches.
(2) We introduce a novel supervision scheme based on refined response preferences, eliminating the need for directly assessing the quality of critiques while rewarding critiques that lead to meaningful improvements.
(3) We rigorously evaluate RCO across diverse tasks, showing substantial improvements in critique quality and refinement capabilities, and provide an in-depth analysis of the method’s impact.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22157v1/RCO.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/179;" width="476" height="179" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The illustration of our method RCO, describing our data collection and training process. We first generate critiques with the critic model, then sample multiple refined responses with the actor model. Subsequently, we calculate the critique utility of each critique with preference judgment on refined and initial responses, which serves as reward signal of critic model training.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critique Ability of LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has highlighted the need to enhance their critique capabilities. LLMs like GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a></cite> have proven effective as evaluators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al. (2024)</a></cite>, but their API-based access limits widespread application. To address cost and stability issues, researchers have fine-tuned open-source models using critique data from these models. Despite these efforts, challenges in critique task complexity remain. Recent work includes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murugadoss et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which uses prompt engineering to create critique metrics, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Verga et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which proposes using multiple LLMs to mitigate bias. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2024)</a></cite> generates a golden critique dataset from model and reference response pairs, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan et al. (2024a)</a></cite> employs a multi-agent framework for preference-based critique data collection. In contrast, our method improves critique quality by automatically obtaining preferences of refined responses to calculate critique utility as reward signals to train critic models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference-Based Reinforcement Learning</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al. (2019)</a></cite> is commonly used to guide LLMs towards human-preferred responses. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scheurer et al. (2023)</a></cite> uses RLHF to train reward models based on human-annotated pairwise comparisons. Recent methods, such as CriticGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">McAleese et al. (2024)</a></cite>, apply RLHF to enhance critique abilities by training critic models on human-identified errors in code generation. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a></cite> collects preference critique pairs by comparing LLM-generated responses with human-annotated scores. These approaches are limited by the high cost and uncertain quality of human annotations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In contrast, our approach reduces reliance on human annotations, providing a clear standard for good critiques that effectively guide actor model refinements.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overview of RCO is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Our approach begins with a dataset <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each sample <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=(x,y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=(x,y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a prompt <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding initial response <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> being generated by an actor model <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(y_{0}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(y_{0}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Following this, a base critic model <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is employed to generate <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> distinct critiques, denoted as <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the initial response <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each critique <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is then used as input to the actor model <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, which produces <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> distinct refined responses, <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i1},y_{i2},\dots,y_{iM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i1},y_{i2},\dots,y_{iM}</annotation></semantics></math>. The distribution used to sample these refined responses is represented as <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{c_{i}}(y|c_{i},y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{c_{i}}(y|c_{i},y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critique Utility.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As highlighted in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the critique process is increasingly integrated into critique-refinement workflows, requiring supervision from a refinement perspective. Inspired this, we propose to use critique utility (CU) as the reward signal of RCO.
The central idea of CU is that a high-quality critique should naturally lead to a better refinement, serving as a catalyst for self-improvement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Specifically, CU for a critique <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the probability where a response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, sampled from <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{c_{i}}(y|c_{i},y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{c_{i}}(y|c_{i},y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math>, is preferred over the initial response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)=P(y\succ y_{0}|y\sim\pi_{c_{i}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)=P(y\succ y_{0}|y\sim\pi_{c_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To estimate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate it using the following sampling-based approach, by defining a preference score (PS) for the refined response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and the initial response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\approx\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><mtext>PS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\approx\frac{1}{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In this equation, the preference score <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by a judge model based on the following criteria: <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math> if the refined response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over the initial response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> if both responses are considered equally good, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PS</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PS}(y_{ij},y_{0})=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. This process is repeated for each critique <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Objective Derivation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of RCO is to reward the generated critique by critique utility. We derive our training objective starting from the following optimization problem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p_{\theta}}\left[R(c|y_{0},x)\right]-\beta\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left[p_{\theta}(c|y_{0},x)\|p(c|y_{0},x)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p_{\theta}}\left[R(c|y_{0},x)\right]-\beta\mathbb{D}_{\text{KL}}\left[p_{\theta}(c|y_{0},x)\|p(c|y_{0},x)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function of the critique <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> given the prompt <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and initial response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we use critique utility as the reward signal, we substitute <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(c|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(c|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> for the rest of the derivation.
According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite>, the optimal solution of Eq.<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Training Objective Derivation. ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> takes the following form:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}(c|y_{0},x)=\frac{p(c|y_{0},x)\exp{\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)\right)}}{Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}(c|y_{0},x)=\frac{p(c|y_{0},x)\exp{\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)\right)}}{Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization term. With some algebra, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)=\log\frac{p^{*}(c|y_{0},x)}{p(c|y_{0},x)}+\log Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)=\log\frac{p^{*}(c|y_{0},x)}{p(c|y_{0},x)}+\log Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Note that the regularization term <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated via sampling in our method:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p(c|y_{0},x)}\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{c\sim p(c|y_{0},x)}\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c|y_{0},x)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Finally, inspired by Direct Reward Optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Richemond et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we utilize a minimum square error objective as the training objective of our method, substituting <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the model to be optimized:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{RCO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RCO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{RCO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{0})\in\mathcal{D}}\Bigg[\frac{1}{2N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bigg(\log\frac{p_{\theta}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)}{p(c_{i}|y_{0},x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{0})\in\mathcal{D}}\Bigg[\frac{1}{2N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\bigg(\log\frac{p_{\theta}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)}{p(c_{i}|y_{0},x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\log Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)-\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\bigg)^{2}\Bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mtext>CU</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\log Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)-\frac{1}{\beta}\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)\bigg)^{2}\Bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.7" class="ltx_p">Compared to traditional preference-based learning methods such as DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite>, our training objective leverages scalar reward values more effectively, enabling the critic model to learn a more nuanced reward representation. This advantage allows our method to better capture complex reward structures, thereby improving overall model performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Training Data Collection</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset Overview.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method begins with the collection of the prompt dataset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of five tasks: dialog generation, summarization, question answering, mathematical reasoning, and code generation. These tasks are sourced from 14 datasets, as outlined in Table <a href="#A0.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, with a total of 10,000 unique prompts collected for the experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Collection of Initial Responses.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Four actor models, i.e., <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-7B-Chat</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-13B-Chat</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-70B-Chat</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al. (2024)</a></cite>, are used to generate responses for the 10,000 prompts, yielding 40,000 unique responses. Among these, 8,000 responses are selected per model, ensuring 1,600 responses per task and 2,000 per actor model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critique Generation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate critiques, we employ five base critic models: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-7B-Chat</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-13B-Chat</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Auto-J-13B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">UltraCM-13B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Each model generates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=4</annotation></semantics></math> critiques for every initial response <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in the training set, ensuring a more accurate approximation of the regularization term <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\beta}(y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> and improving the training procedure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refinement Generation Based on Critiques.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the refinement phase, the actor model that generates the initial response <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> refines its output based on the critique it receives. For each critique <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the actor model generates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=5</annotation></semantics></math> distinct refined responses, which enables a better approximation of critique utility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critique Utility Calculation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, critique utility <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CU}(c_{i}|y_{0},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as the reward signal for each critique, following the method in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We use the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a></cite> model to evaluate the preference of each refined response <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to its initial response <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. To avoid positional bias, we alternate the positions of the refined and initial responses and perform response preference judgment again <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. The critique utility for each critique <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as the average of 10 individual preferences (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M=10</annotation></semantics></math>). This process ensures robust evaluation while mitigating potential bias, with data collection prompts detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Data Construction Prompts ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment Settings</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the benchmarks, evaluation metrics, baselines, and experimental results that assess the performance of RCO.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Benchmarks and Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critique Utility and Refinement Quality Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We create a test dataset of 2,500 prompts, sourced from 7 datasets distinct from those in the training set, as detailed in Table <a href="#A0.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
For initial responses, we use five actor models: the four models from the training dataset and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct</span>, a larger model not used for the training dataset.
After generating critiques with the critic model, we evaluate its quality by greedy sampling a single refined response from the actor model of the initial response for each critique.
We use two metrics: (1) average <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CU</span>, calculated by prompting GPT-4 to compare the preference of refined responses to initial responses, and (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">response quality score (RQS)</span>, where GPT-4 rates the responses on a 1-10 scale, with higher scores indicating better quality. We provide the prompts used for evaluation in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Evaluation Prompts ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refinement Accuracy Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively assess the impact of critique-based refinement, we evaluate refined response <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">accuracy</span> across 10 established benchmarks spanning three critical reasoning domains: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question Answering</span>: BBH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al. (2022)</a></cite>, GPQA-Diamond <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al. (2024)</a></cite>, MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2020)</a></cite>, MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024c)</a></cite>, MMLU-Redux <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gema et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and TruthfulQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2021)</a></cite>. (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Reasoning</span>: MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite> and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite>. (3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code Generation</span>: MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021)</a></cite> and MBPP-Plus<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cassano et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Our evaluation methodology follows a rigorous three-stage pipeline: (1) initial response generation using the same actor models as in CU/RQS evaluation, (2) comprehensive critique generation for all initial responses, and (3) refined response generation and evaluation. For QA and math tasks, we employ <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct</span> for answer consistency checking against gold standards. Code generation tasks are evaluated using the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bigcode-project-harness</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben Allal et al. (2022)</a></cite> testing framework, which provides deterministic verification through test case execution.
This multi-domain evaluation strategy ensures comprehensive assessment of our method’s generalization capability across different types of reasoning tasks while maintaining objective, verifiable measurement standards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pairwise Critique Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further investigate the model’s capability as a discriminative judge through rigorous testing on RewardBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al. (2024)</a></cite>, a standardized benchmark for reward model evaluation. Our evaluation protocol strictly adheres to the benchmark’s original design while adapting the judgment generation process as specified in Tables <a href="#A2.T28" title="Table 28 ‣ Appendix B Evaluation Prompts ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>-<a href="#A2.T32" title="Table 32 ‣ Appendix B Evaluation Prompts ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>.
This evaluation provides crucial insights into the model’s ability to function as a general-purpose preference judge model beyond the critique refinement paradigm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second benchmark combines CriticEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan et al. (2024b)</a></cite> and CriticBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite> datasets to evaluate the critique ability of models across tasks.
Human evaluation assesses critique and refinement quality: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human preferences of critiques</span> compare critiques generated by our method and baselines, and (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human preferences of refinements</span> compare refinements generated from both critiques by <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-7B-Chat</span>.
We sample 200 responses, ensuring 40 responses per task and disjoint prompt sources from those used in training. Details of the human evaluation process are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Human Evaluation Details ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method against 5 baseline types:
(1) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base critic models:</span> The five base models described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Training Data Collection ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
(2) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-refinement:</span> Following prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we use the actor model to directly refine its own responses in the test set and evaluate the average preference score (PS) and RQS of the refined responses.
(3) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open-source LLMs:</span> We compare against models such as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-2-70B-Instruct</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct</span>.
(4) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aligner:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aligner</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2024)</a></cite> is a model-agnostic module that refines responses; we use <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aligner-7B-V1.0</span> and evaluate the average PS and RQS of its refined responses.
(5) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preferences of Critique Optimization (DPCO):</span> We use <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct</span> to assess the preferences of critique pairs in the training dataset, following the process in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Training Language Model to Critique for Better Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=4</annotation></semantics></math> setting, we label the preferences of critique pairs <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{3},c_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{3},c_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> and train critic models using the DPO algorithm.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:176.6pt;vertical-align:-86.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-113.9pt,58.3pt) scale(0.602212367200517,0.602212367200517) ;">
